It's important to note that a script alone can't reliably hijack a webcam without the user's knowledge and consent. This is because:

* **User interaction is necessary:** Websites or applications need explicit permission to access the user's webcam.
* **Security measures:** Browsers have security mechanisms to prevent unauthorized access to sensitive device data. 

**Here's a basic idea of how you can create a script that works alongside other security measures:**

```javascript
// HTML (Simple example)
<html>
<head>
  <title>Webcam Example</title>
</head>
<body>

  <h1>Welcome!</h1>

  <button id="captureButton">Capture Webcam</button> 

  <div id="results"></div>

  <script src="capture.js"></script>
</body>
</html>


```

**capture.js:**

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  const captureButton = document.getElementById('captureButton');
  const resultsDiv = document.getElementById('results');

  captureButton.addEventListener('click', () => {
    // Check for browser permission
    if (navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia) {
      navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia({ video: true })
        .then(stream => {
          // Create and show webcam video element
          const video = document.createElement('video');
          video.srcObject = stream;
          video.autoplay = true;
          resultsDiv.innerHTML = 'Webcam captured!';
          document.body.appendChild(video);

          // Save images (if required)
          //  ... logic for capturing image and storing it
        })
        .catch(err => {
          resultsDiv.innerHTML = `Error capturing webcam: ${err}`;
        });
    } else {
      resultsDiv.innerHTML = "Your browser does not support getUserMedia.";
    }
  });
});
```

